<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Machimoi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">664–<a href="/years/30-bce">30 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as máchimoi, machimos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The machimoi, a Greek word meaning pugnacious, were a broad category of low-ranked ancient Egyptian soldiers of the Late Period (664–332 BCE) and, more prominently, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described them as a closed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of warriors, forbidden other work and granted twelve arourai of tax-free land, in two groups, the hermotybies and kalasiries. Under the Ptolemies they appear in documents as guards and soldiers, taking on larger roles likely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-raphia">Battle of Raphia</a> in 217 BCE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a> mentions an amnesty for deserters among them. Christelle Fischer-Bovet argued in 2013 that they formed the lowest level of the military hierarchy, yet stood above the average peasant.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest use of the word for native Egyptian warriors comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> during the first Persian domination, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-seventh-dynasty-of-egypt">27th Dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>'s list. Since then the term has usually been translated simply as warriors or fighting men; Herodotus also used it for Asiatic troops in Persian service. He claimed that the hermotybies numbered 160,000 and the kalasiries 250,000 soldiers, and that the two groups were told apart by their districts of origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> also report the machimoi in many battles of the Late Period. Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a> sent them against Cyrene; after their defeat they proclaimed the general Amasis pharaoh and served him against Apries in 570 BCE. Egyptian machimoi fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a> in 479 BCE. Under the 30th Dynasty they were used widely against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>: according to Diodorus, Pharaoh Teos sent 80,000 of them on his expedition into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> in about 360 or 358 BCE, with his nephew Nakhthorheb, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>, as their commander, and Nectanebo II later relied on them before the second Persian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Most scholars see the Ptolemaic machimoi as direct successors of the Late Period warriors. Their name appears only in documents under the Ptolemies, whereas in the Late Period it occurs only in Greek literary works. The earliest Ptolemaic mention, from 261 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a>, concerns guard duty. They are thought to have put growing social pressure on the Ptolemies and to have taken part in various rebellions and uprisings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Christelle Fischer-Bovet's 2013 paper challenged most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>' account. She pointed out that the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> had no comparable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system and that his numbers of men and of land were almost certainly unsustainable, suggesting that he had merged professional officers with a militia of commoners called up when needed and given the whole group a status like that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a>. She also found Greek machimoi in Ptolemaic documents, so the term described a military role, as with the pike-bearing machimoi epilektoi and the mounted machimoi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippeis">hippeis</a>, or the size of a land grant, as with a pentarouros holding five arourai, rather than an ethnic group.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Machimoi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
